Review #849A
===========================================================================

Paper summary
-------------
Submission #849 offers a proof that a provided variant of double-RLPN can solve the binary decoding problem by way of a reduction from the dual attack to the learning parity with noise LPN problem.  The key advances offered in the paper include 1) the observation that an analysis of properties of the score function used in the algorithm allows a proof that doesn't rely on the type of assumptions that previous work in this direction required, 2) an update due to their tweak of the rate regime in which the proof is valid and 3) an analysis of the new approach that shows that its performance is only diminished by a polynomial up to polynomial factors.  The authors also offer an extremely brief discussion of the possibility of moving this approach across norms to apply to lattice attacks, however, this portion of the work seems extremely underdeveloped.

Strengths and weaknesses
------------------------
Strengths:  The main contribution of the work is relevant and of interest for the community.  The approach is very reasonable.  Weaknesses:  The paper seems a bit hastily put together to me.  It has many issues in its presentation including the lack of key definitions that are required for the paper to act as a cohesive exposition of the topic.  A component offered as a contribution in the introduction is not addressed in sufficient detail in the paper, though there is still space available in the submission.

Detailed review text
--------------------
Submission #849 offers a proof that a provided variant of double-RLPN can solve the binary decoding problem by way of a reduction from the dual attack to the learning parity with noise LPN problem.  The key advances offered in the paper include 1) the observation that an analysis of properties of the score function used in the algorithm allows a proof that doesn't rely on the type of assumptions that previous work in this direction required, 2) an update due to their tweak of the rate regime in which the proof is valid and 3) an analysis of the new approach that shows that its performance is only diminished by a polynomial up to polynomial factors.  The authors also offer an extremely brief discussion of the possibility of moving this approach across norms to apply to lattice attacks, however, this portion of the work seems extremely underdeveloped.

The approach that the authors employ is to focus on the second-order behavior of the score function, specifically they analyze the spread of the score function values.  This analysis allows the procedure to guess in polynomial time of a noise component with reasonably good probability using a coordinate flipping gadget.  The success analysis depends entirely on this second-order behavior, and thus removes the need for heuristics.

The approach is quite reasonable in my opinion and the topic is definitely of interest.  I believe that this type of work is valuable and will support further advances in this direction.  There are a few issues that I have that make me not support the paper as much as I would hope.

First, it seems to me that the paper feels a bit hastily written.  There are a very large number of typos and issues that seem a bit inappropriate for a finished work.  I have listed a few examples below in comments, but I saw more in the paper.  I would make an exhaustive list, but it seems a bit unfair to me to do so when it seems the not a sufficient amount of care was taken to produce a final product for the PC consideration.  Another issue, which is minor, is that there is as much attention paid to the idea of extending these ideas to the LWE setting in the introduction as in the body of the text.  This portion of the work is not really developed in any reasonable detail, so perhaps it should be more of a remark than a promise of a contribution.

In the end, I have mixed feelings about the paper and I will mark it as borderline in my list.  Please find below a list of detailed observations about the paper.

Comment: The abbreviation LPN is never defined.  While anyone working in this area knows learning parity with noise, this should be defined for completeness and accessibility for the entire community.  Actually, I am realizing now that the same applies for RLPN.  Some new to this area may be introduced to Dual Attack 3.0 through this work, so it should provide the proper foundation.

Comment: page 3 starts with an unwanted indentation in the middle of a sentence.  To avoid this issue, remove extra carriage returns after the equation environment.

Typo: page 5: "variantof"

Omission: page 7: "i) a guess can be tested in polynomial and" seems to be missing "time".

Typo: page 7: "expect" -> "except"

Typo: page 7: Your double quotation marks on the word "flipped" are not input properly for latex.  You should use double directed single quotes, i.e. ``flipped''.

Comment: page 7: Again, I see an indentation in the middle of a sentence.  Please check everywhere that you have no blank lines in your source after equation environments.  LaTeX is designed to treat these as paragraph breaks and it causes many spacing and flow problems to have these breaks in unwanted places.

Comment: page 15: There is a sentence in the middle of the page that overflows the margins.  I'm not sure that restricting the expression to be unbroken by line breaks will actually fix the issue.  Perhaps the authors could consider adding some specificity to the description by writing something similar to, "To work, the procedure \decodeddualvectors\ uses two subroutines: ..."  In the preamble the authors could define
\newcommand{\decodeddualvectors}{\mbox{\sc Decoded-Dual-Vectors}}
to ensure there are no line breaks or additional hyphenations in the name of the algorithm.  Similar care would need to be given to the mathematical expressions in the line to avoid unwanted line breaks, but such macros should resolve any other issues with the procedure names at least.

Missing reference: Page 29 has a broken citation link.


