Review #849B
===========================================================================

Paper summary
-------------
The paper devises a variant of the doubleRLPN dual attack for the binary decoding problem whose analysis does not rely on any heuristic model — notably not on the Poisson weight-enumerator model that underpins both the TCC 2023 corrected RLPN analysis and the Eurocrypt 2024 doubleRLPN paper. The main idea is pleasingly simple: flip coordinates of the noisy codeword one at a time, observe how the LPN score function reacts, and use the direction of change to guess whether each coordinate belongs to the error. Because each coordinate only requires distinguishing two distributions (flipped vs. unflipped), a second-order Chebyshev bound plus a union bound suffices — there is no need to control the full tail of the score function across the entire secret space. The authors claim performance matching the original doubleRLPN up to polynomial factors for rates R < 0.5, covering the regime where dual attacks beat ISDs. They also replace the sublinear-block juxtaposition code of [CDMT24] with a constant-block version decoded via syndrome tables, which tightens the superpolynomial loss down to polynomial.

Strengths and weaknesses
------------------------
Strengths:

Removing the Poisson model is a genuinely important goal. The heuristic status of existing dual-attack analyses has been a sore point ever since the Ducas–Pulles controversy, and a clean proof — even for a variant — would be a real contribution. The bit-flipping idea is clever and makes the proof conceptually much simpler than the tail-estimation route.

The proof is organized in a modular way that I found easy to follow at the high level: a signal lemma (the bias gap in Lemma 7.3), a noise lemma (the Chebyshev-based concentration in Proposition 7.1), and an instantiation lemma (Proposition 7.5 for the juxtaposition code). Each piece has a clear role.

The discussion in Section 8.2 drawing parallels with lattice-based dual attacks is brief but thought-provoking. I think this could genuinely guide future work on proving attacks like those in [CMST25].

Weaknesses:

The paper promises full rigor but does not quite deliver it. Multiple proofs assert key steps as "straightforward" or defer them to Taylor-expansion hand-waves. For a paper whose entire raison d'être is removing heuristics, this is a real problem — readers and cryptographers evaluating security claims need to be able to trust the proofs end-to-end.

The polynomial overhead is never pinned down. The paper shows existence of some poly-bounded loss but does not bound its degree, and the step that would show the asymptotic exponent is preserved (the O(log² n) adjustment to w) is explicitly left unproved.

Some scope claims are overstated, particularly around R ≤ 0.5 "naturally" satisfying all the new constraints.

Detailed review text
--------------------
(A) The bit-flipping signal (Lemma 7.3).

This is the lemma that makes the whole approach work: it says that δ_w^{(n−s)}(u−1) − δ_w^{(n−s)}(u) is at least (1/poly(n)) · δ_w^{(n−s)}(u), i.e., adjacent Krawtchouk biases differ by a polynomial fraction of themselves. The claim is believable and the recurrence in Lemma 10.5 is the right starting point.

That said, I found a clear error in the proof (Appendix 10.2). Lemma 10.5 gives the difference as (2w/(n−s)) · δ_{w−1}^{(n−s−1)}(u−1), but when the authors apply it in the proof of Lemma 7.3, they write the difference as just δ_{w−1}^{(n−s−1)}(u−1) — the factor 2w/(n−s) has vanished. Now, in the constant-rate regime the authors care about, w = Θ(n−s) so this factor is Θ(1) and can be absorbed into f_1 without issue. But as written, the equation is wrong, and the authors should fix it and state explicitly that 2w/(n−s) ≥ 1/poly(n) under their parameter assumptions.

More seriously, the next step — going from δ_{w−1}^{(n−s−1)}(u−1) to Ω̃(δ_w^{(n−s)}(u)) — is handled in two sentences citing "differentiability of κ on A along with Taylor's theorem." This is exactly where the proof needs to be careful. The authors need to verify that the shifted normalized pair ((w−1)/(n−s−1), (u−1)/(n−s−1)) stays strictly inside the non-oscillatory region A, with a margin that does not shrink faster than 1/n. The condition Root(K_w^{(n−s)}) − u ∈ Ω(n−s) should give this, but the conversion from a linear gap at the integer level to a uniform interior margin at the normalized level is not written out. For a fully rigorous paper, it has to be.

(B) Variance bounds (Lemma 10.1, Proposition 7.1).

The strategy here — decompose Var(F) ≤ V + C, observe that the covariance terms vanish when h_N ≠ g_N (because distinct nonzero vectors over F_2 give independent uniform shifts via R), and bound the remaining h_N = g_N terms by how many auxiliary-code blocks overlap — is the right idea. I do not see a structural error in the approach.

I do have several concerns about the execution. First, the independence argument is stated too loosely. The reason the h_N ≠ g_N covariance vanishes is that (h_N R^T, g_N R^T) is jointly uniform when h_N, g_N are linearly independent over F_2, which follows from Lemma 10.3. This is the core technical point — it is what distinguishes this second-moment method from the broken independence assumptions of earlier work — and it deserves more than a passing reference.

Second, there appears to be a typo in the statement of Lemma 10.4: the variance bound involves 2^{s^{(i)} − t_{aux}^{(i)}} in the denominator, but Lemma 2.9 applied to the auxiliary code gives 2^{s^{(i)} − k_{aux}^{(i)}} (the codimension). Looking at the proof of Lemma 10.1 (which uses this result), the k_{aux}-based quantity is what actually appears. So this seems to be a typo in the lemma statement, not a mathematical error — but it is an unfortunate one in a paper about rigor.

Third, the compression from Lemma 10.4 to Lemma 10.1 introducing the n^{b+1} factor is not adequately explained. The authors themselves seem unsure about it (Remark 10.2 conjectures it should really be O(2^b)). Since the theorem only needs a polynomial bound, the looseness is tolerable, but the intermediate steps should be spelled out.

(C) Juxtaposition codes (Proposition 7.5).

The idea of using b = ⌈s/k_{aux}⌉ blocks with syndrome-table decoding is natural and the sample-count part of Proposition 7.5 (comparing products of blockwise binomial coefficients to the global one) is a direct application of Proposition 2.14. I have no issues there.

The bias-product comparison is harder: it amounts to showing that ∑_j κ(ω_j, τ_j) is close to κ(ω, τ) when the (ω_j, τ_j) are the blockwise normalized parameters and (ω, τ) is the global one. For constant b this should follow from a mean-value argument on κ, but the proof just says "straightforward using Proposition 2.14 and Proposition 2.15" without doing it. Given that the analogous Taylor step in Lemma 7.3 is already under-justified, I think the authors need to write this out.

I also want to flag that the entire juxtaposition-code story only works because b is constant, which is only guaranteed under the assumption s/k_{aux} ∈ O(1). The paper acknowledges this but sometimes writes as if the polynomial-loss result is more general than it is.

(D) Constraints in Theorem 4.1.

The starred constraints are consistent and I checked that they do not contradict each other for the parameter sets in [CDMT24]. A few remarks on scope:

The information-set condition n − s − k ∈ ω(1) is automatic when R < 0.5, since n − s − k ≥ (n − 2k) + (k − s) and n − 2k = Ω(n) when R is bounded away from 1/2. At R = 0.5 exactly, this becomes k − s ∈ ω(1), which is not guaranteed just by s ≤ k. So the paper's "up to rate R ≤ 0.5" is slightly misleading — R strictly less than 0.5 is the clean regime.

The constraint s/k_{aux} ∈ O(1) has nothing to do with the code rate; it comes from the auxiliary-code design. The paper's discussion lumps it together with the R ≤ 0.5 story in a way that overstates how automatic it is.

Neither of these is a correctness issue — the constraints are what they are — but the paper should be more precise about what is assumed vs. what follows from the rate condition.

(E) Polynomial overhead.

The final time and memory formulas look right in shape. My issue is not with the formulas themselves but with the gap between what is proved and what is claimed. The proof produces a sample threshold N ≥ f(n^b) / (δ · δ_{aux})^2 where f = (f_0 f_1)^2 f_2^3 is some poly-bounded function whose degree is never specified. The paper then asserts (end of Section 4) that regardless of the polynomial, one can recover the original asymptotic exponent by nudging w upward by O(log² n), and that this "could rigorously be shown" by differentiating the relevant asymptotic expression. But it is not shown.

For a paper about any other topic, this would be a minor loose end. For a paper whose headline is "fully proved without models," it is a significant omission: it is precisely the step that turns "polynomial overhead exists" into "the provable variant matches the heuristic one in the exponent." I would like to see this argument made explicit, or at minimum a concrete bound on the degree of the overhead.

Questions for authors
---------------------
1. In the proof of Lemma 7.3, the factor 2w/(n−s) from Lemma 10.5 disappears. I assume this is absorbed into f_1, but can you confirm and state the needed lower bound on w/(n−s) explicitly? More importantly, can you write out the Taylor/MVT argument showing that ((w−1)/(n−s−1), (u−1)/(n−s−1)) remains uniformly interior to A? This is the crux of the lemma and currently it is a two-sentence sketch.
    
2. The displayed variance bound in Lemma 10.4 has 2^{s^{(i)} − t_{aux}^{(i)}} in the denominator whereas Lemma 2.9 gives 2^{s^{(i)} − k_{aux}^{(i)}}. Is this a typo? The proof of Lemma 10.1 seems to use the k_{aux} version, so I think it is, but please clarify.
    
3. Can you supply the actual proof of Proposition 7.5 for the bias-product inequality? Specifically, the comparison of ∑_j κ(ω_j, τ_j) with κ(ω, τ) for constant b — this needs a mean-value argument on κ, not just a reference to differentiability.
    
4. At the end of Section 4 you write that increasing w by O(log^2 n) suffices to absorb the polynomial overhead, and that this "could rigorously be shown." Can you either provide this argument or give an explicit bound on the degree of f(n^b)? Without this, the paper proves polynomial overhead exists but does not confirm the exponent is preserved.


